\newlabel{fig:pdchaptercoolingapparatusphotos}{{1.8}{30}{(a) The photodiode was affixed to this aluminium block. The power resistors provided heating to the block and the copper rod provided a thermal link between the liquid nitrogen bath and the block. (b) A photodiode glued to the block such that a large proportion of the photodiode was in contact with it.\relax }{figure.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1.7}The Saturation Limit of Some Laser Components Extended InGaAs Photodiodes}{30}{section.1.7}}
\newlabel{sec:pdchapterlasercomponentssaturation}{{1.7}{30}{The Saturation Limit of Some Laser Components Extended InGaAs Photodiodes}{section.1.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.9}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the dark current for each photodiode at their maximum datasheet specified reverse bias as a function of temperature. The apparatus limited dark current measurements to a couple of nanoamps. Exponentials were fitted to the data. Photodiodes with similar cut-off wavelengths share marker shapes, and 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm ones. The measurement of the DC dark current approached the noise floor of the apparatus around a few nanovolts. Right panel: the dark noise of each photodiode was measured as a function of temperature. The black dashed line indicates the shot noise level for a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. At 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the measurements for the IG24x500S4i did not change much as the temperature changed, there was a knee around this frequency (see Figure \ref  {fig:pdappendixig24temperaturenoisecurrent}); the noise of the IG24x500S4i was $600-700\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\pico \ampere /\sqrt  {\hertz }}$ at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz.\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.47}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterdarkcurrenttermperaturealldiodes}{{1.9}{31}{Left panel: the dark current for each photodiode at their maximum datasheet specified reverse bias as a function of temperature. The apparatus limited dark current measurements to a couple of nanoamps. Exponentials were fitted to the data. Photodiodes with similar cut-off wavelengths share marker shapes, and 1\,mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\,mm ones. The measurement of the DC dark current approached the noise floor of the apparatus around a few nanovolts. Right panel: the dark noise of each photodiode was measured as a function of temperature. The black dashed line indicates the shot noise level for a 10\,mA current. At 10\,Hz, the measurements for the IG24x500S4i did not change much as the temperature changed, there was a knee around this frequency (see Figure \ref {fig:pdappendixig24temperaturenoisecurrent}); the noise of the IG24x500S4i was $600-700\,\si {\pico \ampere /\sqrt {\hertz }}$ at 10\,Hz.\relax }{figure.caption.47}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.10}{\ignorespaces The saturation limit of three Laser Components photodiodes was measured. The photodiodes were biased so that their noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz was equal to the shot noise of a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. The IG24x500S4i and IG26x500S4i were exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 220\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \metre } and the IG22x1000S4i was exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 1000\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \metre }. The size of the light modulation whose relative size was known was tracked as the DC photocurrent was increased. As the photodiodes saturated, the relative size of the AC signal decreased. These photodiodes had low saturation limits compared to the FD10D, which showed no signs of saturation up to 16.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA (see Figure \ref  {fig:pdchapterfd10dacdc}).\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.48}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterlasercomponentsratio}{{1.10}{32}{The saturation limit of three Laser Components photodiodes was measured. The photodiodes were biased so that their noise at 10\,Hz was equal to the shot noise of a 10\,mA current. The IG24x500S4i and IG26x500S4i were exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 220\,\si {\micro \metre } and the IG22x1000S4i was exposed to a beam whose $3\sigma $ width was 1000\,\si {\micro \metre }. The size of the light modulation whose relative size was known was tracked as the DC photocurrent was increased. As the photodiodes saturated, the relative size of the AC signal decreased. These photodiodes had low saturation limits compared to the FD10D, which showed no signs of saturation up to 16.5\,mA (see Figure \ref {fig:pdchapterfd10dacdc}).\relax }{figure.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1.8}Excess Noise in the Photocurrent made by the IG24x500S4i}{33}{section.1.8}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{33}{section*.49}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{33}{section*.52}}
\newlabel{eq:pd_chapter_ideality_factor}{{1.4}{33}{}{equation.1.8.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces The noise of a 1.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA photocurrent made by the IG24x500S4i (blue) exceeded the expected amount (red) based on Equation \ref  {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} by a factor of $1.75$. Note that the height of the 1111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz peak cannot be expressed in terms of amplitude spectral density units; however, this peak was measured to be the right level and so this beam was not saturating the photodiode. \relax }}{34}{figure.caption.50}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchaptershotnoiseproblemig24}{{1.11}{34}{The noise of a 1.3\,mA photocurrent made by the IG24x500S4i (blue) exceeded the expected amount (red) based on Equation \ref {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} by a factor of $1.75$. Note that the height of the 1111\,Hz peak cannot be expressed in terms of amplitude spectral density units; however, this peak was measured to be the right level and so this beam was not saturating the photodiode. \relax }{figure.caption.50}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.12}{\ignorespaces A noise projection measurement was performed to determine if the excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i (see Figure \ref  {fig:pdchaptershotnoiseproblemig24}) was due to bias noise. The left hand panels show the bode plot of the transfer function between the photodiode's cathode and the output of the transimpedance amplifier when the photodiode was producing 1.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA of photocurrent. The right hand panel shows the effect of subtracting bias noise from the signal at the output of the transimpedance amplifier. The peak at 1121\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz was a modulation applied to the photodiode's cathode; 42.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}dB of this signal was removed by using the noise projection technique which is in close agreement with the amount expected from the measured transfer function. However, the level of the broadband noise remained unchanged thus indicating that the excess noise witnessed in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i was not due to bias noise.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.51}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapternoiseprojection}{{1.12}{35}{A noise projection measurement was performed to determine if the excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i (see Figure \ref {fig:pdchaptershotnoiseproblemig24}) was due to bias noise. The left hand panels show the bode plot of the transfer function between the photodiode's cathode and the output of the transimpedance amplifier when the photodiode was producing 1.3\,mA of photocurrent. The right hand panel shows the effect of subtracting bias noise from the signal at the output of the transimpedance amplifier. The peak at 1121\,Hz was a modulation applied to the photodiode's cathode; 42.5\,dB of this signal was removed by using the noise projection technique which is in close agreement with the amount expected from the measured transfer function. However, the level of the broadband noise remained unchanged thus indicating that the excess noise witnessed in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i was not due to bias noise.\relax }{figure.caption.51}{}}
\citation{Beattie1962}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{36}{section*.55}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1.9}Properties of an InSb detector}{36}{section.1.9}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.13}{\ignorespaces To investigate possible reasons why there was excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i, its ideality factor was measured (red points). Equation \ref  {eq:pd_chapter_ideality_factor} was used to do an unweighted fit to the data ($I_0 = (360\pm 10)\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {nA}$ and $n = 1.02\pm 0.01$). The IG24x500S4i was found to be close to ideal as $n$ was close to unity and so this does not provide clues to the source of excess noise in the photocurrent.\relax }}{37}{figure.caption.53}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterforwardbiasiv}{{1.13}{37}{To investigate possible reasons why there was excess noise in the photocurrent produced by the IG24x500S4i, its ideality factor was measured (red points). Equation \ref {eq:pd_chapter_ideality_factor} was used to do an unweighted fit to the data ($I_0 = (360\pm 10)\,\si {nA}$ and $n = 1.02\pm 0.01$). The IG24x500S4i was found to be close to ideal as $n$ was close to unity and so this does not provide clues to the source of excess noise in the photocurrent.\relax }{figure.caption.53}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.14}{\ignorespaces Different light levels were shone onto the photodiode and the current produced at 0.05\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V reverse bias was recorded. These measurements are shown by the stars. Based on the model obtained from the data shown in Figure \ref  {fig:pdchapterforwardbiasiv}, the current flowing through the photodiode was predicted (indicated by the lines). The model and the measurements are in agreement with each other although the photodiode may have been saturating for the 3.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mW measurement.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.54}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterforwardbiasmodelwithlight}{{1.14}{38}{Different light levels were shone onto the photodiode and the current produced at 0.05\,V reverse bias was recorded. These measurements are shown by the stars. Based on the model obtained from the data shown in Figure \ref {fig:pdchapterforwardbiasiv}, the current flowing through the photodiode was predicted (indicated by the lines). The model and the measurements are in agreement with each other although the photodiode may have been saturating for the 3.3\,mW measurement.\relax }{figure.caption.54}{}}
\citation{Kimukin2003}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.15}{\ignorespaces The P5968 InSb detector had its cathode grounded and the noise of the photocurrent due to the blackbody radiation produced by room temperature aluminium was measured (red). The expected level of photocurrent was calculated to be 5.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \ampere } and the measured photocurrent was 5.3\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \ampere }. The noise level of the photocurrent corresponds to shot noise (green). The noise of the transimpedance amplifier (blue) is well below the shot noise of the photocurrent.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.57}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterp5968noise}{{1.15}{39}{The P5968 InSb detector had its cathode grounded and the noise of the photocurrent due to the blackbody radiation produced by room temperature aluminium was measured (red). The expected level of photocurrent was calculated to be 5.5\,\si {\micro \ampere } and the measured photocurrent was 5.3\,\si {\micro \ampere }. The noise level of the photocurrent corresponds to shot noise (green). The noise of the transimpedance amplifier (blue) is well below the shot noise of the photocurrent.\relax }{figure.caption.57}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{39}{section*.56}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.16}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the DC dark current of the P5968 as a function of bias. Middle panel: $1/f$ noise rose as the bias of the P5968 was increased (see Figure \ref  {fig:pdchapterinsbnoiseasfunctionofbias}). Right panel: At a frequency where $1/f$ was not present, the noise of the photocurrent was measured. The excess noise factor is the multiplicative factor that the shot noise corresponding to the DC photocurrent needs to be multiplied by to get the noise level that was measured.\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.59}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterinsbbiased}{{1.16}{40}{Left panel: the DC dark current of the P5968 as a function of bias. Middle panel: $1/f$ noise rose as the bias of the P5968 was increased (see Figure \ref {fig:pdchapterinsbnoiseasfunctionofbias}). Right panel: At a frequency where $1/f$ was not present, the noise of the photocurrent was measured. The excess noise factor is the multiplicative factor that the shot noise corresponding to the DC photocurrent needs to be multiplied by to get the noise level that was measured.\relax }{figure.caption.59}{}}
